{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "To protect computers against malware, the following controls should be implemented: (1) Implement signature-based or non-signature-based malicious code protection mechanisms at system entry and exit points to detect and eradicate malicious code; (2) Automatically update malicious code protection mechanisms as new releases are available in accordance with organizational configuration management policy and procedures; and (3) Configure malicious code protection mechanisms to perform periodic scans of the system and real-time scans of files from external sources as the files are downloaded, opened, or executed.",
  "citations": ["SI-3"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "To limit what each user is allowed to do, the following controls address this question: [AC-3(4)] Discretionary Access Control, [AC-7] Unsuccessful Logon Attempts, [SC-43] Usage Restrictions, and [CM-5(6)] Limit Library Privileges.",
  "citations": ["AC-3(4)", "AC-7", "SC-43", "CM-5(6)"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "Yes, new employees should be screened before they start. This is addressed by the [PS-3] Personnel Screening control, which requires screening individuals prior to authorizing access to the system.",
  "citations": ["PS-3"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "Changes to systems' settings are controlled through the following controls: CM-3 (Configuration Change Control), CM-6 (Configuration Settings), CM-3(2) (Testing, Validation, and Documentation of Changes), CM-6(1) (Automated Management, Application, and Verification), SA-10(7) (Security and Privacy Representatives), CM-2(7) (Configure Systems and Components for High-risk Areas), CM-3(7) (Review System Changes), and SA-10 (Developer Configuration Management).",
  "citations": ["CM-3", "CM-6", "CM-3(2)", "CM-6(1)", "SA-10(7)", "CM-2(7)", "CM-3(7)", "SA-10"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "The number of failed sign-in attempts before locking an account is defined by the organization and is specified in the [AC-7] control. The exact number is [Assignment: organization-defined number].",
  "citations": ["AC-7"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "To keep systems' clocks in sync so log times are right, the following controls should be implemented: (1) Synchronize system clocks within and between systems and system components as specified in SC-45. (2) Compare internal system clocks with an authoritative time source and synchronize when the time difference exceeds the defined period as specified in SC-45(1). (3) Use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for audit records and ensure they are in Coordinated Universal Time or include the local time offset as specified in AU-8. (4) Ensure audit records are in a standardized format and time-correlated as specified in AU-12(1) and AU-12(2).",
  "citations": ["SC-45", "SC-45(1)", "AU-8", "AU-12(1)", "AU-12(2)"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "Yes, the incident response plan should be tested. This is addressed by multiple controls including [IR-3] Incident Response Testing, [IR-3(1)] Automated Testing, [IR-3(3)] Continuous Improvement, and [IR-3(2)] Coordination with Related Plans.",
  "citations": ["IR-3", "IR-3(1)", "IR-3(3)", "IR-3(2)"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "Yes, a warning message should be shown before people sign in, as addressed by the [AC-12(3)] Timeout Warning Message control, which requires displaying an explicit message to users indicating that the session will end in a defined time period. This control is relevant to informing users about session expiration before they sign in.",
  "citations": ["AC-12(3)"]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "answer": "To notice someone attacking our network, we can use the following controls: [AT-2(4)] Suspicious Communications and Anomalous System Behavior, [SI-4(14)] Wireless Intrusion Detection, [SI-4(16)] Correlate Monitoring Information, and [SI-4(7)] Automated Response to Suspicious Events.",
  "citations": ["AT-2(4)", "SI-4(14)", "SI-4(16)", "SI-4(7)"]
}

